  and [1m[38;5;153mnotifications[39m is read-only with respect to tasks[0m. The first is what makes the event log trustworthy; the second is
  what keeps the feed from becoming a second source of truth (the failure mode that sank approach C).

  [1mBuild order.[0m Notifications can't be first — they query a log that doesn't exist. Three pieces, each its own
  spec→plan→implement cycle:

  1. [1mTask core[0m — model, store, [38;5;153mapplyTaskChange[39m chokepoint, event log, rendered list. [3mNo notification behavior[0m, but the
     log gets written from day one. Without this there is nothing to notify about.
  2. [1mNotification engine[0m — sweep, feed query, read state. Entirely headless and unit-testable against a synthetic log;
     no UI needed to prove it correct.
  3. [1mNotification UI[0m — bell, badge, feed panel, star toggle, due-date editing.

  This session's spec covers the notifications design (2 and 3) and pins the constraints that piece 1 must satisfy — the
  chokepoint, the event shape, the actor field. That way task core gets built notification-ready instead of being
  retrofitted.

  One thing to settle now, because it's cheapest before any code exists:

